{"code":"HA_ERR_AUTOINC_ERANGE","errno":167,"level":"error","message":"Database query error: Out of range value for column 'auditid' at row 1","sql":"UPDATE medicalrecords SET complaints = 'The patient, a 35-year-old male, presents with complaints of recurrent headaches, nausea, and dizziness that have been persistent over the past week. The headaches are described as a dull, throbbing pain mostly concentrated in the frontal and temporal regions of the head. The pain intensifies during periods of stress, and the patient reports that it becomes more pronounced in the late afternoon. Additionally, he experiences intermittent nausea, often accompanied by dizziness, particularly when standing up quickly or after long periods of sitting. These episodes of dizziness have made it difficult for him to maintain his regular daily activities. The patient has also noticed that the intensity of the headaches has gradually increased, and the frequency has gone from once or twice a week to almost daily. He expresses concern that these symptoms are interfering with his work and social life, as he finds it difficult to concentrate during meetings and often has to leave social events early due to the discomfort. The symptoms have not been relieved by over-the-counter pain medications such as ibuprofen or acetaminophen, and he is seeking a professional evaluation to determine the underlying cause of his condition.\\n\\nhpc:', hpc = '\\nSure! Here\\'s an expanded version of each field, with at least 200 words for each:\\n\\ncomplaints:\\nThe patient, a 35-year-old male, presents with complaints of recurrent headaches, nausea, and dizziness that have been persistent over the past week. The headaches are described as a dull, throbbing pain mostly concentrated in the frontal and temporal regions of the head. The pain intensifies during periods of stress, and the patient reports that it becomes more pronounced in the late afternoon. Additionally, he experiences intermittent nausea, often accompanied by dizziness, particularly when standing up quickly or after long periods of sitting. These episodes of dizziness have made it difficult for him to maintain his regular daily activities. The patient has also noticed that the intensity of the headaches has gradually increased, and the frequency has gone from once or twice a week to almost daily. He expresses concern that these symptoms are interfering with his work and social life, as he finds it difficult to concentrate during meetings and often has to leave social events early due to the discomfort. The symptoms have not been relieved by over-the-counter pain medications such as ibuprofen or acetaminophen, and he is seeking a professional evaluation to determine the underlying cause of his condition.\\n\\nhpc:\\nThe patient reports that his headache episodes have been present for the last two weeks but have worsened significantly in the past week. The headaches typically start in the late afternoon, often after long hours of work at his desk. The pain is described as throbbing and intermittent, which the patient states is exacerbated by bright lights and loud noises. He also mentions that stress seems to worsen the symptoms, especially during hectic work days. In addition to headaches, he experiences nausea that occurs alongside the pain and dizziness that occurs when he stands up quickly. There is no clear pattern to when these symptoms appear, although they tend to follow stressful situations at work. The patient denies any recent head trauma or injuries, but he does report that he has had trouble sleeping lately due to the pain. Despite trying multiple over-the-counter medications, such as ibuprofen and paracetamol, his symptoms have not improved. He has also attempted rest and hydration but has not found significant relief. The patient denies any visual disturbances, but he does report occasional blurry vision during episodes of dizziness. The symptoms have led him to seek medical attention to determine whether there is an underlying medical condition that may be contributing to his headaches and dizziness.', pmh = 'The patient has a significant past medical history of hypertension, which has been managed with medication for the past five years. He has been taking Lisinopril 10 mg daily, as prescribed by his primary care physician, and reports good compliance with his medication regimen. His blood pressure readings have been stable, with an average of 130/85 mmHg, according to his recent home monitoring. In addition to hypertension, the patient has a history of seasonal allergies, for which he takes antihistamines during peak allergy seasons. He denies any history of major surgeries or hospitalizations. The patient also reports occasional episodes of mild back pain, which he attributes to his sedentary work environment. He has no known history of heart disease, diabetes, or chronic respiratory conditions such as asthma or COPD. The patient has never experienced any significant mental health issues such as anxiety or depression, though he does experience stress related to his job. He does not smoke, and he consumes alcohol in moderation, about two to three drinks per week. The patient has not received any recent vaccinations and has not had a flu shot in the past year. He is due for a routine check-up, but the recent onset of headaches and dizziness prompted him to seek medical attention sooner.', sh = 'The patient works as a senior office manager in a corporate setting. His job involves long hours at a desk, managing emails, meetings, and administrative tasks. The patient describes his work environment as high-pressure, with frequent deadlines and the need to multitask throughout the day. He reports that his work often leads to high levels of stress, which he acknowledges may be contributing to his current symptoms. His daily routine consists of sitting at a desk for extended periods, often over 8 hours a day, without much physical movement. The patient recognizes that this sedentary lifestyle may be affecting his overall health. He tries to walk during lunch breaks but admits to not engaging in regular exercise outside of work. His diet consists primarily of fast food and takeout due to his busy schedule. While he does make an effort to drink water throughout the day, he acknowledges that he often forgets to stay properly hydrated. The patient also reports insufficient sleep, often averaging only 5-6 hours a night due to his demanding work schedule. He admits that his stress levels increase during periods of work overload, but he rarely takes breaks or uses relaxation techniques. Despite the stressful nature of his job, he enjoys the challenges it provides and feels fulfilled by his role, although he is concerned that the ongoing symptoms may be affecting his ability to perform at his best.', odq = '\\nSure! Here\\'s an expanded version of each field, with at least 200 words for each:\\n\\ncomplaints:\\nThe patient, a 35-year-old male, presents with complaints of recurrent headaches, nausea, and dizziness that have been persistent over the past week. The headaches are described as a dull, throbbing pain mostly concentrated in the frontal and temporal regions of the head. The pain intensifies during periods of stress, and the patient reports that it becomes more pronounced in the late afternoon. Additionally, he experiences intermittent nausea, often accompanied by dizziness, particularly when standing up quickly or after long periods of sitting. These episodes of dizziness have made it difficult for him to maintain his regular daily activities. The patient has also noticed that the intensity of the headaches has gradually increased, and the frequency has gone from once or twice a week to almost daily. He expresses concern that these symptoms are interfering with his work and social life, as he finds it difficult to concentrate during meetings and often has to leave social events early due to the discomfort. The symptoms have not been relieved by over-the-counter pain medications such as ibuprofen or acetaminophen, and he is seeking a professional evaluation to determine the underlying cause of his condition.\\n\\nhpc:\\nThe patient reports that his headache episodes have been present for the last two weeks but have worsened significantly in the past week. The headaches typically start in the late afternoon, often after long hours of work at his desk. The pain is described as throbbing and intermittent, which the patient states is exacerbated by bright lights and loud noises. He also mentions that stress seems to worsen the symptoms, especially during hectic work days. In addition to headaches, he experiences nausea that occurs alongside the pain and dizziness that occurs when he stands up quickly. There is no clear pattern to when these symptoms appear, although they tend to follow stressful situations at work. The patient denies any recent head trauma or injuries, but he does report that he has had trouble sleeping lately due to the pain. Despite trying multiple over-the-counter medications, such as ibuprofen and paracetamol, his symptoms have not improved. He has also attempted rest and hydration but has not found significant relief. The patient denies any visual disturbances, but he does report occasional blurry vision during episodes of dizziness. The symptoms have led him to seek medical attention to determine whether there is an underlying medical condition that may be contributing to his headaches and dizziness.\\n\\npmh:\\nThe patient has a significant past medical history of hypertension, which has been managed with medication for the past five years. He has been taking Lisinopril 10 mg daily, as prescribed by his primary care physician, and reports good compliance with his medication regimen. His blood pressure readings have been stable, with an average of 130/85 mmHg, according to his recent home monitoring. In addition to hypertension, the patient has a history of seasonal allergies, for which he takes antihistamines during peak allergy seasons. He denies any history of major surgeries or hospitalizations. The patient also reports occasional episodes of mild back pain, which he attributes to his sedentary work environment. He has no known history of heart disease, diabetes, or chronic respiratory conditions such as asthma or COPD. The patient has never experienced any significant mental health issues such as anxiety or depression, though he does experience stress related to his job. He does not smoke, and he consumes alcohol in moderation, about two to three drinks per week. The patient has not received any recent vaccinations and has not had a flu shot in the past year. He is due for a routine check-up, but the recent onset of headaches and dizziness prompted him to seek medical attention sooner.\\n\\nsh:\\nThe patient works as a senior office manager in a corporate setting. His job involves long hours at a desk, managing emails, meetings, and administrative tasks. The patient describes his work environment as high-pressure, with frequent deadlines and the need to multitask throughout the day. He reports that his work often leads to high levels of stress, which he acknowledges may be contributing to his current symptoms. His daily routine consists of sitting at a desk for extended periods, often over 8 hours a day, without much physical movement. The patient recognizes that this sedentary lifestyle may be affecting his overall health. He tries to walk during lunch breaks but admits to not engaging in regular exercise outside of work. His diet consists primarily of fast food and takeout due to his busy schedule. While he does make an effort to drink water throughout the day, he acknowledges that he often forgets to stay properly hydrated. The patient also reports insufficient sleep, often averaging only 5-6 hours a night due to his demanding work schedule. He admits that his stress levels increase during periods of work overload, but he rarely takes breaks or uses relaxation techniques. Despite the stressful nature of his job, he enjoys the challenges it provides and feels fulfilled by his role, although he is concerned that the ongoing symptoms may be affecting his ability to perform at his best.\\n\\nodq:\\nThe patient denies any known drug allergies and reports that he has not had any allergic reactions to medications in the past. He has been taking Lisinopril 10 mg daily for hypertension without any side effects. The patient is not currently taking any other prescription medications but does occasionally take over-the-counter medications such as ibuprofen for pain relief. He does not use any herbal supplements or alternative therapies. In terms of immunizations, the patient is up to date with his routine vaccinations, although he admits that he has not had the flu shot in the past year. He has not experienced any adverse reactions to vaccines or other medications. The patient reports that he occasionally experiences mild seasonal allergies, especially during the spring and fall, but he manages this with over-the-counter antihistamines. He denies any history of anaphylaxis or other severe allergic reactions. The patient is also not taking any recreational drugs and reports that he has never been involved in substance abuse. He does not engage in smoking or vaping, and his alcohol consumption is limited to occasional social drinking. The patient reports that his overall health is generally stable, and he has not experienced any significant health issues in recent years, aside from the hypertension.', dhx = 'The patient has no known family history of neurological disorders or chronic illnesses such as migraines, epilepsy, or stroke. However, his maternal uncle has a history of frequent migraines, which have been a recurring issue for him since his early adulthood. The patient’s father has a history of hypertension, which was diagnosed at the age of 50 and managed with medication. His mother has also been diagnosed with hypertension, which is managed with a different class of medication. There is no known history of heart disease, diabetes, or any other chronic illnesses in the patient’s immediate family. The patient’s siblings are generally healthy, with no reported health conditions. There is no family history of mental health disorders such as depression or anxiety. The patient does not report any genetic conditions or inherited diseases in his family history. He is unaware of any environmental factors that could be contributing to his current symptoms, but he does recall that several members of his extended family have had issues with high blood pressure, which might indicate a genetic predisposition to hypertension in the family. The patient’s family has a history of longevity, with both his parents living well into their 80s without significant chronic health issues.', se = 'The patient denies any shortness of breath, chest pain, or palpitations at rest or during physical activity. He reports no recent episodes of syncope or near-syncope. His cardiovascular system appears to be functioning normally, and he has not experienced any swelling of the legs or feet. The patient also denies any recent weight loss, fever, or chills. He has not experienced any significant changes in appetite or sleep patterns outside of his headaches and dizziness. There is no history of gastrointestinal symptoms such as nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea. The patient’s musculoskeletal system appears to be functioning without major issues, although he occasionally experiences mild back pain, likely due to his sedentary work lifestyle. He reports no joint swelling, stiffness, or tenderness. The patient denies any recent rashes, skin lesions, or other dermatological issues. He has not had any recent infections, and his immune system appears to be functioning normally. The patient’s neurological symptoms are limited to headaches and dizziness, without any other focal neurological deficits such as weakness, numbness, or difficulty speaking. Overall, the patient reports feeling generally well aside from the recent onset of his symptoms, and there is no indication of systemic illness.', fh = 'The patient’s father, aged 62, has a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus, which was diagnosed at the age of 55. His father has been managing his condition with oral hypoglycemic agents and lifestyle modifications. The patient’s mother, aged 60, has a history of hypertension, which has been managed with medication for the past 10 years. Both parents have a history of sedentary lifestyles, with limited physical activity and poor dietary habits. The patient’s siblings are all in good health, with no known chronic conditions. The patient’s paternal grandparents had a history of cardiovascular disease, including heart attacks and strokes, which has raised the patient’s awareness of his own health risks. The patient’s maternal grandmother had a history of breast cancer, but the patient does not have any direct concerns about his family history of cancer at this time. There are no reported mental health issues in the family, and the patient’s extended family has generally been free of major chronic conditions. The patient acknowledges that his family history may influence his own health risks and plans to take preventive measures to maintain a healthy lifestyle moving forward.', obsGynecHistory = 'The patient is a 30-year-old female who is G2P2 (Gravida 2, Para 2), meaning she has been pregnant twice and delivered two children. Her last delivery was 2 years ago, and both pregnancies were uncomplicated. She had vaginal deliveries, and there were no significant complications during the pregnancies or postpartum period. The patient reports having regular menstrual cycles, with her last period occurring 3 weeks ago, and the flow was normal, lasting about 5 days. She denies any history of gynecological disorders, such as endometriosis, polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), or fibroids. The patient has not experienced any abnormal vaginal bleeding, pelvic pain, or abnormal discharge. She is not currently using any form of contraception, as she and her partner are not planning for additional children at this time. The patient is not experiencing any issues with urinary or bowel function. She is in good overall health and does not have any concerns related to her reproductive or gynecological health. The patient reports being up to date with her routine gynecological examinations, including Pap smears and breast exams, and she has no history of abnormal results.', oe = 'Upon examination, the patient appears alert and oriented, with no signs of distress. His vital signs are as follows: blood pressure 130/80 mmHg, pulse 76 beats per minute, respiratory rate 16 breaths per minute, and temperature 98.6°F. The patient’s general appearance is well-groomed, and there is no visible discomfort or pain. The head and neck examination reveals no abnormalities, and there is no evidence of trauma or swelling. The neurological examination shows no signs of focal deficits. Cranial nerve testing is intact, and the patient demonstrates normal sensation, strength, and coordination in all four limbs. His reflexes are within normal limits, and there is no signs of ataxia or dysmetria. The cardiovascular examination reveals a regular heart rate and rhythm, with no murmurs, gallops, or rubs. The lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally, with no wheezing, crackles, or rhonchi. Abdominal examination is unremarkable, with no tenderness, masses, or organomegaly. The musculoskeletal exam reveals no signs of swelling or deformities, and the patient demonstrates a full range of motion in all joints. The fundoscopic exam reveals no abnormalities in the retina or optic disc. Overall, the patient’s physical examination does not suggest any acute or severe underlying conditions but does warrant further investigation into the possible causes of his headaches and', record_type = 'Old Record', patientid = '10', consulationby = 3 WHERE id = 1","sqlMessage":"Out of range value for column 'auditid' at row 1","sqlState":"22003","stack":"Error: Out of range value for column 'auditid' at row 1\n    at PromisePoolConnection.query (C:\\Programming\\PLS-DIFFERENT-VERSIONS\\LobnosProfessionalVersion\\lobnos-server\\node_modules\\mysql2\\lib\\promise\\connection.js:29:22)\n    at promisifyQuery (C:\\Programming\\PLS-DIFFERENT-VERSIONS\\LobnosProfessionalVersion\\lobnos-server\\dist\\hooks\\helper.js:84:43)\n    at process.processTicksAndRejections (node:internal/process/task_queues:95:5)\n    at async PrimeMedicalRecords.updateRecord (C:\\Programming\\PLS-DIFFERENT-VERSIONS\\LobnosProfessionalVersion\\lobnos-server\\dist\\models\\MedicalRecords\\medicalRecordClass.js:40:46)\n    at async updateAConsultationRecord (C:\\Programming\\PLS-DIFFERENT-VERSIONS\\LobnosProfessionalVersion\\lobnos-server\\dist\\controllers\\medicalRecords\\medicalRecordsHandler.js:72:33)"}
